A race condition is an issue that occurs when two or more threads access shared resources simultaneously and can cause inconsistencies in the outcome of their operations. This happens because each thread has its own copy of the data being accessed, but they may not be synchronized properly.

Here's a simple example:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int x = 10;

void increment(int* ptr) {
    *ptr++ = x;
}

int main() {
    int* ptr = malloc(sizeof(int));

    printf("Before incrementing\n");
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
        increment(&ptr[i]);
        printf("%d ", *ptr[i]);
    }
    printf("\nAfter incrementing\n");

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, two threads, `thread_a` and `thread_b`, both try to increment the same variable `x`. Since `ptr` is a pointer to a local variable, it only holds the value of `x` at the time of allocation. However, due to the lack of synchronization between the threads, they both modify the same memory location, causing the values stored in `ptr` to become inconsistent.

When `thread_a` increments `ptr[0]`, it sets `ptr[0]` to `x`, which becomes `10`. Then, when `thread_b` increments `ptr[0]`, it also sets `ptr[0]` to `10`, effectively overwriting the previous value. The output will show `10` instead of `9`.

To fix this problem, you need to ensure that all threads have exclusive access to the shared resource (`ptr`) using synchronization mechanisms like locks or atomic operations.
